1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,940
Washington says it controls the Strait of

2
00:00:00,840 --> 00:00:05,500
Hormuz. Iran says it's closed. So, is it

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,439
open? Depends who you ask. The oil tankers?

4
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:09,318
Just... sort of parked.

5
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:11,720
Tankers are waiting at anchor instead of

6
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:14,839
sailing. Iran closed the strait soon after

7
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:17,260
US-Israeli strikes began in late February.

8
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:19,820
So Washington says they have total control

9
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:22,440
over the strait. But Iran flatly denies

10
00:00:22,339 --> 00:00:22,740
it.

11
00:00:22,947 --> 00:00:25,927
Exactly. Tehran insists the waterway stays

12
00:00:25,827 --> 00:00:28,267
closed until Washington changes its behaviour.

13
00:00:28,609 --> 00:00:31,589
And if they do reopen it? They want guarantees

14
00:00:31,489 --> 00:00:32,029
first.

15
00:00:32,503 --> 00:00:35,283
Iran's security chief outlined seven conditions.

16
00:00:35,903 --> 00:00:38,423
Lifting sanctions, releasing frozen assets,

17
00:00:38,683 --> 00:00:41,203
and a wider ceasefire in Gaza and Lebanon.

18
00:00:41,653 --> 00:00:44,153
Before the war, 130 ships crossed daily.

19
00:00:44,293 --> 00:00:46,753
Now? Just eight on a recent Tuesday. So,

20
00:00:46,953 --> 00:00:49,553
open? Eight very brave ships would say yes.

21
00:00:49,979 --> 00:00:52,879
So ships are basically ghosting the strait.

22
00:00:53,359 --> 00:00:55,479
Is that a blockade or just extreme caution?

23
00:00:55,864 --> 00:00:58,524
Both. Attacks and harassment continue. Iran

24
00:00:58,424 --> 00:01:00,804
and the US maintain overlapping blockades

25
00:01:00,704 --> 00:01:01,324
right now.

26
00:01:01,489 --> 00:01:03,570
So the world's most important oil lane is

27
00:01:03,469 --> 00:01:06,189
basically on hold. Great news for anyone

28
00:01:06,090 --> 00:01:07,349
who likes paying for petrol.

29
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:10,780
The conflict is spreading. The Bab al-Mandeb

30
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:13,939
Strait, near Yemen and Saudi Arabia, now

31
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:15,099
faces similar threats.

32
00:01:15,439 --> 00:01:18,239
On Tuesday a US Navy helicopter fired on

33
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:20,619
a ship near Yemen, and Houthi attacks have

34
00:01:20,519 --> 00:01:22,439
now killed sailors for the first time in

35
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:22,839
this war.

36
00:01:23,156 --> 00:01:25,516
Oman and Pakistan are pushing hard to reopen

37
00:01:25,416 --> 00:01:27,876
the waterway, passing messages between both

38
00:01:27,776 --> 00:01:28,236
sides.

39
00:01:28,525 --> 00:01:31,165
Iran presented a proposal in mid-September

40
00:01:31,065 --> 00:01:34,664
and received a US reply. But progress is

41
00:01:34,564 --> 00:01:34,905
minimal.

42
00:01:35,148 --> 00:01:37,648
And mediators say a draft deal to reopen

43
00:01:37,548 --> 00:01:39,728
the strait is now in its final stage.

44
00:01:40,073 --> 00:01:42,613
The timeline is brutal. War starts late

45
00:01:42,513 --> 00:01:45,453
February. Ceasefires break down repeatedly.

46
00:01:45,913 --> 00:01:47,673
Talks stall over nuclear programs.

47
00:01:48,066 --> 00:01:50,526
Iran calls the strait an unbreakable red

48
00:01:50,426 --> 00:01:53,526
line. Any US interference triggers immediate

49
00:01:53,426 --> 00:01:54,126
retaliation.

50
00:01:54,564 --> 00:01:57,204
Tehran demands the US end aggression first.

51
00:01:57,644 --> 00:01:59,544
They will not resume talks while strikes

52
00:01:59,444 --> 00:01:59,984
continue.

53
00:02:00,245 --> 00:02:02,765
So we have a diplomatic paradox. Both sides

54
00:02:02,665 --> 00:02:04,285
are digging in while the waterway stays

55
00:02:04,185 --> 00:02:04,625
closed.

56
00:02:05,033 --> 00:02:07,853
The fallout is hitting the neighbours. Iran

57
00:02:07,753 --> 00:02:10,933
fired missiles at a US base in Jordan, Kuwait

58
00:02:10,833 --> 00:02:13,893
shot down drones, and Bahrain sounded air

59
00:02:13,793 --> 00:02:14,573
raid sirens.

60
00:02:14,993 --> 00:02:17,173
Trump predicts oil will drop 'like a rock'.

61
00:02:17,633 --> 00:02:20,473
But Brent crude is actually up over 5% this

62
00:02:20,373 --> 00:02:20,713
week.

63
00:02:20,933 --> 00:02:24,313
Geopolitical risk premiums returned. Markets

64
00:02:24,213 --> 00:02:26,833
fear prolonged supply disruption more than

65
00:02:26,733 --> 00:02:27,213
the rhetoric.

66
00:02:27,506 --> 00:02:29,746
So we pay higher gas prices to wait for

67
00:02:29,646 --> 00:02:32,105
a deal that might not happen. Classic economic

68
00:02:32,005 --> 00:02:32,526
strategy.

69
00:02:32,943 --> 00:02:35,783
Energy prices reflect the tension. Brent

70
00:02:35,683 --> 00:02:39,303
crude holds at $88.55. US West Texas Intermediate

71
00:02:39,203 --> 00:02:41,623
sits at $82.26.

72
00:02:42,226 --> 00:02:44,246
The core of the deadlock remains unresolved.

73
00:02:44,886 --> 00:02:46,606
It's a bit like two people holding the same

74
00:02:46,506 --> 00:02:48,046
door shut. From opposite sides.

75
00:02:48,486 --> 00:02:50,906
A draft agreement focuses on restoring maritime

76
00:02:50,806 --> 00:02:53,506
movement. But Iran wants a voluntary fund,

77
00:02:53,786 --> 00:02:55,166
not mandatory tolls.

78
00:02:55,299 --> 00:02:57,558
So Gulf states and European countries would

79
00:02:57,458 --> 00:02:59,459
pitch in for navigation support instead.

80
00:02:59,999 --> 00:03:01,238
Interesting legal workaround.

81
00:03:01,617 --> 00:03:04,197
Exactly. It avoids international law issues

82
00:03:04,097 --> 00:03:06,177
while keeping the strait open for commercial

83
00:03:06,077 --> 00:03:06,677
traffic.

84
00:03:07,003 --> 00:03:09,243
The proposed fund would replace formal fees

85
00:03:09,143 --> 00:03:11,403
with contributions for environmental protection

86
00:03:11,303 --> 00:03:12,503
and search-and-rescue.

87
00:03:12,763 --> 00:03:15,063
Mediators say a draft deal to reopen the

88
00:03:14,963 --> 00:03:17,903
strait is ready. Getting both sides to sign

89
00:03:17,803 --> 00:03:18,863
it is the hard part.

90
00:03:19,123 --> 00:03:21,223
The nuclear program remains a main point

91
00:03:21,123 --> 00:03:23,503
of dispute. Washington wants it addressed

92
00:03:23,403 --> 00:03:23,843
first.

93
00:03:24,072 --> 00:03:26,712
Tehran insists the strait and security come

94
00:03:26,612 --> 00:03:29,971
before nuclear talks. The sequence is non-negotiable.

95
00:03:30,196 --> 00:03:32,616
Mediators are urging everyone to stop the

96
00:03:32,516 --> 00:03:34,396
strikes and get back to technical talks.

97
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:37,780
The war's economic toll is staggering. US

98
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:41,280
military spending alone has reached $47.1

99
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:43,260
billion as of October.

100
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:46,100
Houthi attacks in Bab al-Mandeb cause the

101
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,860
first fatalities since the war began. Saudi

102
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:51,060
Arabia announces a new maritime alliance.

103
00:03:51,433 --> 00:03:54,273
So we have sinking ships, rescue crews under

104
00:03:54,173 --> 00:03:57,013
fire, and a new coalition forming just to

105
00:03:56,913 --> 00:03:57,753
keep lanes open.

106
00:03:58,048 --> 00:04:00,888
Maritime security is fragmenting. Nations

107
00:04:00,788 --> 00:04:02,908
are building parallel systems because the

108
00:04:02,808 --> 00:04:03,968
old frameworks failed.

109
00:04:04,217 --> 00:04:06,437
I suppose insurance premiums just wrote

110
00:04:06,337 --> 00:04:08,957
a check to both sides. Efficient market

111
00:04:08,857 --> 00:04:09,457
correction.

112
00:04:09,893 --> 00:04:12,293
The US Treasury Secretary says a deal could

113
00:04:12,193 --> 00:04:14,833
come within days. Tehran says not until

114
00:04:14,733 --> 00:04:17,513
its conditions are met. So, is a deal close?

115
00:04:18,513 --> 00:04:19,933
Again... depends who you ask.

116
00:04:20,253 --> 00:04:22,473
The US says a deal to reopen the strait

117
00:04:22,373 --> 00:04:25,333
could come by Wednesday. Iran wants guarantees

118
00:04:25,233 --> 00:04:25,753
first.

119
00:04:26,042 --> 00:04:29,142
So Washington expects speed, Tehran demands

120
00:04:29,042 --> 00:04:31,822
patience. Both sides are preparing for war

121
00:04:31,722 --> 00:04:32,601
while negotiating.

122
00:04:32,979 --> 00:04:35,739
Diplomacy is rarely a straight line. Both

123
00:04:35,639 --> 00:04:37,659
sides want a win they can sell at home.

124
00:04:37,936 --> 00:04:41,176
So, is the Strait of Hormuz open? Washington

125
00:04:41,076 --> 00:04:44,356
says yes, Tehran says no, and the tankers

126
00:04:44,256 --> 00:04:45,936
are still waiting for a second opinion.

127
00:04:46,330 --> 00:04:49,010
If the deal falls through, we face prolonged

128
00:04:48,910 --> 00:04:51,910
confrontation. Oil prices will stay volatile.

129
00:04:52,220 --> 00:04:54,880
Or Washington and Tehran finally find a

130
00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:57,360
compromise over the fund model. The draft

131
00:04:57,260 --> 00:04:58,200
is ready.

132
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:00,760
Either way, we'll keep paying for uncertainty

133
00:05:00,660 --> 00:05:03,400
until the paperwork finally clears. Standard

134
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:03,840
procedure.

135
00:05:10,176 --> 00:05:10,576
Until Washington and Tehran bridge their

136
00:05:10,476 --> 00:05:10,976
gap over the Strait of Hormuz, global markets

137
00:05:10,876 --> 00:05:12,276
will remain on high alert. Thanks for watching

138
00:05:12,176 --> 00:05:13,916
— subscribe for more explainers.
